<section>
    <h2>Setting the table once</h2>
    <p>
        Most tests need something ready before they start: a user, a basket, a record in the database. Playwright
        Test gives you hooks for it. <code>test.beforeEach</code> runs before every test.
        <code>test.beforeAll</code> runs once per worker. And <code>globalSetup</code> runs once for the whole run,
        before any worker starts.
    </p>
<pre><code>// report-setup.ts: runs once, in the main process
import { request } from '@@playwright/test';

export default async function globalSetup() {
  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: process.env.DUNGEON_URL ?? 'https://playwright-dungeon-crawl.onrender.com' });
  const reply = await api.post('/api/reports');
  process.env.REPORT_ID = (await reply.json()).id;   // the workers inherit this
  await api.dispose();
}</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Workers are processes.</strong> Each worker runs its own <code>beforeAll</code>, so with five
        workers it runs five times. Anything that must happen exactly once goes in <code>globalSetup</code>, and a
        short value like an id reaches the workers through <code>process.env</code>.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Clean hands</h2>
    <p>
        Every test already gets a fresh browser context through the <code>page</code> fixture: its own cookies, its
        own storage, nothing left over from the test before. Keep it that way. A test that depends on what another
        test left behind passes on its own and fails in a crowd.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Side by side</h2>
    <p>
        The kit runs one worker, so rooms take turns on the small dungeon server. For parallel tests, give them a
        config of their own that builds on the kit's:
    </p>
<pre><code>// parallel.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from '@@playwright/test';
import base from './playwright.config';

export default defineConfig(base, {
  globalSetup: './report-setup.ts',
  testMatch: '**/*.parallel.ts',   // only these files, so the normal run never picks them up
  workers: 5,
  fullyParallel: true,             // tests in the same file may run at the same time
});</code></pre>
<pre><code>npx playwright test -c parallel.config.ts</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>fullyParallel.</strong> Without it, Playwright keeps a file's tests together, in order, in one
        worker. Five workers and one file would still mean one test at a time.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Severing Pit</h2>
    <p>
        The hydra has five heads, and they only rear when a crawler stands at every one. Then all five have to fall
        within three seconds, or they grow back. Start one hunt, then send five crawlers, each with clean hands, all
        at once. One after another never works: the hydra won't wait ten seconds for the rest, and a crawler already
        at the pit can't face a second head.
    </p>
</section>
